# What does NOT go into the build context.
#
# There was no such file. `COPY . .` in the builder stage therefore copied the
# whole working tree — and the tree on the server contains `deploy/backups/`,
# which is thirty gzipped dumps of the production database. Every build packed
# the guests of the last month into a layer, and the daemon had to receive them
# over the socket before it could even start. On a developer machine the same
# line sends `.next/` (nine gigabytes here) and `node_modules/` (one), which are
# then thrown away by the very next instruction, because the builder takes
# node_modules from the `deps` stage and rebuilds `.next` itself.
#
# So this file is not a tidiness measure. It is the difference between a build
# context of a few megabytes and one that carries the database off the machine.

# Rebuilt inside the image; taking the host's copy is how a stale build wins.
node_modules
.next
out
dist

# Database dumps and volume archives. Never in an image.
deploy/backups

# The live SQLite database and guest uploads — volumes at runtime, not content.
data
public/uploads
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Secrets. `deploy/env.prod` holds APP_SECRET_KEY and the Postgres password.
.env
.env.*
deploy/env.prod
deploy/env.beta
deploy/env.*.local

# History and CI configuration: the build reads neither.
.git
.github
.gitignore

# Local noise.
.DS_Store
*.log
npm-debug.log*
coverage
playwright-report
test-results
.vscode
.idea
deploy/rclone.conf
